CORRECT — every dir-EX release leaves disk==incore (removal durably applied). The dangling dirent is a **post-release async clobber**: a later write (xfsaild destaging a stale cached dir buffer) reintroduces the removed dirent. This definitively rules out the heavyweight release-drain fixes (Case A) and points at the **lightweight xfsaild dir-write ABA suppressor** — no per-handoff latency, so no RULE-0 timeout. Major narrowing. Let me record this pivotal finding.
Case B pinned and recorded. The reintroducer is a stale cached dir buffer being async-destaged. One lightweight, no-rebuild hypothesis: `dir_release_stale=1` (invalidate the demoting node's dir buffers on release — clears flags, no FUA latency, unlike relverify). Let me test it alone at 16 (3rd distinct fix attempt) — checking it doesn't timeout AND reduces the dangling-dirent rate.
`dir_release_stale=1` alone: still leaks (`node8_file22 not gone`, 15/16) but **no timeout** (218s) — so it's lightweight but ineffective. Since the demoter invalidating its OWN buffers didn't help, the reintroducer is a **peer's** stale cached dir buffer (or a path release_stale misses). That's my **3rd refuted fix** (after MHT-up, all-guards). The proven next step is the write-side probe (rebuild) to name the exact reintroducing write. Let me record this narrowing and update tasks.
